{"name":"Planter","hex":"#339900","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"planter","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who plants something.\n\n  Any of the early English or Scottish settlers who were given the lands of the dispossessed Irish populace during the reign of Elizabeth I.\n Any member of a similar landed class of (often wealthy) farm owners elsewhere, such as plantation owners whose lands are worked by farm workers, wage slaves, or slaves.","Any of the early English or Scottish settlers who were given the lands of the dispossessed Irish populace during the reign of Elizabeth I."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/planter"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flowerpot","blocks":[{"text":"A flowerpot, planter, planterette or plant pot is a container in which flowers and other plants are cultivated and displayed. Historically, and still to a significant extent today, they are made from plain terracotta with no ceramic glaze, with a round shape, tapering inwards towards the bottom. Flowerpots are now often also made from plastic, metal, wood, stone, or sometimes biodegradable material. An example of biodegradable pots are ones made of heavy brown paper, cardboard, or peat moss in which young plants for transplanting are grown."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/%22Meillandine%22_Rose_in_clay_pot.jpg/330px-%22Meillandine%22_Rose_in_clay_pot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flowerpot","wikidata":"Q869381"},{"title":"Jardiniere","blocks":[{"text":"Jardinière is a French word, from the feminine form of \"gardener\". In English it means a decorative flower box or \"planter\", a receptacle or a stand upon which, or into which, plants may be placed, usually indoors. The French themselves mostly refer to tabletop \"planter\" versions of such receptacles as cachepots (\"hide-pots\"). The French tend to use jardinière for larger outdoor containers for plants, and for raised beds in gardens in some sort of isolated frame, such as a stone wall, especially growing vegetables and herbs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Holdcroft_majolica_fish_and_lilies_jardiniere%2C_naturalistic_style_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Holdcroft_majolica_fish_and_lilies_jardiniere%2C_naturalistic_style_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jardiniere","wikidata":"Q4907676"},{"title":"Planter (farm implement)","blocks":[{"text":"A planter is a farm implement, usually towed behind a tractor, that sows (plants) seeds in rows throughout a field. It is connected to the tractor with a drawbar or a three-point hitch. Planters lay the seeds down in precise manner along rows. Planters vary greatly in size, from 1 row to 54, with the biggest in the world being the 48-row John Deere DB120. Such larger and newer planters comprise multiple modules called row units. The row units are spaced evenly along the planter at intervals that vary widely by crop and locale. The most common row spacing in the United States today is 30 inches."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/JD_71_Flexi_Planter%2C_2_Row.jpg/330px-JD_71_Flexi_Planter%2C_2_Row.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planter_(farm_implement)","wikidata":"Q7201638"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Shelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin%27s_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg/500px-Shelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin%27s_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Shelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin%27s_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg/500px-Shelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin%27s_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Traditional flowerpots in unglazed terracotta in Charles Darwin's laboratory at Down House","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShelves_of_flower_pots_in_Darwin's_laboratory%2C_Down_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1200541.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg/500px-Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg/1280px-Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg/500px-Palazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Terracotta flowerpot in Italy, decorated with festoons","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APalazzo_socci%2C_terrazza%2C_vaso_in_terracotta_con_festoni.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/JiffyPots.JPG/500px-JiffyPots.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/JiffyPots.JPG/1280px-JiffyPots.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/JiffyPots.JPG/500px-JiffyPots.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Peat pots that are biodegradable and may be planted directly into the soil","link":